<$TOP/mkfiles/mkfile.proto

# the goken cross-toolchain itself: used by tests/ (mk objtype=power) and,
# once self-hosting is viable, as CC/AS/LD for the top-level build too.
O=q
CC=qc -0
LD=ql -0 -s -H7
AS=qa
AR=iar

CFLAGS=$CFLAGS_COMMON -I$TOP/include/arch/power

# claude: -0 disables the R0-conventionally-holds-zero optimization --
# on ql, its r0iszero (asmout.c/span.c); on qc, its own identically-
# named R0ISZERO (gc.h: `#define R0ISZERO (debug['0']==0)`), which
# without -0 emits "MOVW $0, mem" stores assuming they assemble via a
# hardware-zero R0 (real for MIPS's $zero, never true for PowerPC's
# R0). Needed on both tools for the same underlying reason: the
# PowerPC Linux syscall ABI requires r0=syscall number, a real,
# possibly-nonzero value, so nothing in this link can assume R0 stays
# zero. Without qc's own -0, linking fails with ql's "illegal
# combination MOVW SCON NONE NONE S*OREG/SAUTO" -- there is no
# PowerPC store-immediate instruction, so once r0iszero-as-source is
# off, ql has nothing left to encode that store with. -H7 selects
# Linux ELF output, same as every other arch's LD here.
#
# claude: -s (strip the symbol table) is load-bearing here, not just
# a size optimization: ql's HEADTYPE 7 asmsym()/asmlc() output
# corrupts the tail of .data once a build carries enough symbols
# (tests/c/float's helloprintf.exe, but not tests/c/mini's or tests/c/
# vlong's smaller ones) -- confirmed by hexdumping the linked ELF's
# .data section directly: a static `int32 fd = 1` read back as 1792
# with the symbol table left in, exactly right with -s. Root cause
# not fully isolated (the ELF phdrs/shdrs computed sizes/offsets that
# looked internally consistent on inspection); vl/il/zl share this
# HEADTYPE 7 machinery and may carry the same latent bug, just
# unexercised by their own tests/c/* content so far. See
# docs/claude_notes/notes_arch_power.txt.

# claude: no ARCHEXTRAOFILES yet (unlike every sibling mkfile here) --
# lib_core/libc has no lib_core/libc/arch/power/ port yet, so nothing
# here builds against it. tests/c/mini's helloc.exe (the first real
# exercise of qc) links its own minimal xwrite/xexit syscall stubs
# instead, no libc needed.
